# Deployment configuration for imap-mcp.
#
# This repository is public, so none of the values below are committed — they
# identify one specific Cloudflare account, zone and Zero Trust tenant, or one
# mailbox. Since the workers merged into one (#34) there is no config
# generator: a fork simply commits its own values. Concretely:
#
#   - The two CLOUDFLARE_* values are read from your shell / CI environment by
#     wrangler itself.
#   - Everything else is runtime configuration for the Worker: add it as a
#     `vars` block to wrangler.jsonc IN YOUR FORK (correct there, never pushed
#     upstream), or set it in the dashboard. Secrets are set with
#     `wrangler secret put <NAME>`, never as vars.
#
# The shapes are declared in src/env.d.ts; this file documents the values.

# ---- Cloudflare account ----

# Your Cloudflare account ID (dashboard -> Workers & Pages -> Account details).
# wrangler reads this from the environment, which is why no `account_id` is
# committed in wrangler.jsonc.
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=

# API token used by `wrangler deploy` from CI, if you deploy that way.
# Not needed for `wrangler deploy --dry-run`, which is all CI runs today.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# ---- Cloudflare Access (Managed OAuth) — the optional upgrade ----
#
# Authentication has two modes (#35), and ACCESS_AUD is the switch. Without
# it, the MCP_API_KEY secret is the credential (see Secrets below) and the
# instance works the moment it deploys. With it, the worker reads
# `ctx.access`, which the Workers runtime populates for a request Access
# authenticated, compares its audience tag against ACCESS_AUD — and REFUSES
# the API key from then on. Precedence, not fallback: a key leaked before the
# upgrade must not remain a bypass of the stronger control.
#
# ORDER MATTERS, and this is also the lockout recovery: create the Access
# application (Worker destination), verify you can authenticate through it,
# and only THEN set ACCESS_AUD. During that window the key still carries you,
# so there is no gap. If you set the audience before the application worked,
# delete ACCESS_AUD — the instance falls straight back to key authentication.
#
# docs/access.md is the step-by-step, and scripts/setup-access.sh walks it
# interactively. The parts that are easy to get wrong:
#
#   - The Access application's destination is the WORKER (Destinations ->
#     "Add Workers" -> imap-mcp), not a public hostname. That covers every
#     route, Custom Domain and workers.dev URL the Worker has -- and it means
#     the worker must be DEPLOYED BEFORE the application is created, since you
#     cannot attach a Worker that does not exist yet.
#   - MANAGED OAUTH IS OFF BY DEFAULT on a self-hosted application, and it is
#     the whole point. Zero Trust -> Access controls -> Applications -> your app
#     -> Advanced settings -> Managed OAuth. Without it a non-browser client
#     gets a 302 it cannot follow instead of a 401 it can act on, and every MCP
#     client simply fails to connect.
#   - The token Managed OAuth issues to the CLIENT is opaque ("oauth:...") and
#     the worker never sees it. Access exchanges it at the edge and hands the
#     result to the worker as `ctx.access`.
#   - Turn OFF "Accept all available identity providers" (it defaults on) and
#     select your identity provider explicitly.
#   - For Claude Code, allow localhost and loopback redirect URIs.
#   - For claude.ai, allow https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback.
#
# No team domain is needed. The worker fetches no JWKS and parses no JWT, so
# there is nothing to point at <team>.cloudflareaccess.com.

# Application Audience (AUD) tag: Zero Trust -> Access -> Applications ->
# your app -> ADDITIONAL SETTINGS -> "Application Audience (AUD) Tag". Hex
# string. (It is on that tab, not Overview.)
#
# It must be THIS application's tag. Access authenticating someone is not the
# same as Access authenticating them for this application, and one Zero Trust
# account can hold many -- another of which may have a far more generous policy.
ACCESS_AUD=

# ---- Storage ----
#
# Nothing to fill in here, but worth knowing before your first deploy.
#
# The D1 binding in wrangler.jsonc carries a database_name ("imap-mcp") and
# deliberately NO database_id -- an id identifies one account, and this repo
# commits none. Wrangler provisions the database on first deploy and writes the
# id back into wrangler.jsonc. In your fork that write-back is correct: keep
# it, and don't push it upstream. Losing it is how a SECOND database gets
# created, so treat your fork's copy as the durable record.
#
# Apply the schema with `pnpm run db:migrate:local` / `db:migrate:remote`.
#
# The two queues (imap-mcp-sync-chunks and its dead-letter queue) are declared
# in wrangler.jsonc by name only and are created by `wrangler deploy`. Queues
# requires a Workers Paid plan.
#
# So is the R2 bucket that holds attachment bytes (imap-mcp-attachments, #9),
# and for the same reason a database_name is committed but no id is: a bucket
# name is scoped to an account rather than identifying one.

# ---- Mailbox ----
#
# Host and port are configuration, not constants: this server is generic and
# only happens to be run against iCloud. IMAP_USER is not a secret but is
# personal, so it is documented here rather than committed.

# e.g. imap.mail.me.com for iCloud
IMAP_HOST=
# 993 (implicit TLS)
IMAP_PORT=993
# Full email address. For iCloud, LOGIN wants the local part only -- see README.
IMAP_USER=

# ---- What the sync path indexes ----
#
# Every one has a default, so the worker runs without them; the first is worth
# choosing deliberately and the rest are the throttles on a backfill.

# Folders to sync, comma-separated. Defaults to Archive, and that default is
# the point: on iCloud the mail is in Archive, not INBOX -- a spike found INBOX
# holding a single-digit message count against Archive's tens of thousands.
# Comma-separated because a folder name can contain almost anything else,
# including the hierarchy delimiter: SYNC_FOLDERS=Archive,Sent,Lists/rust-dev
SYNC_FOLDERS=Archive

# Uids per queue message. Default 100. This is the number that keeps a backfill
# to a few hundred LOGINs instead of tens of thousands: work is chunked by uid
# RANGE, never per message. It is also the bucket size the "what is already
# indexed?" query counts in, so changing it moves every bucket boundary and
# re-enqueues the folder once. The upsert makes that wasteful, not wrong.
SYNC_CHUNK_UIDS=100

# How many messages are fetched in one FETCH. Default 10. This is the count half
# of the peak-memory bound; SYNC_MAX_FETCH_BYTES below is the byte half, and a
# fetch is cut by whichever it reaches first.
SYNC_CHUNK_SIZE=10

# Byte budget for one FETCH, and the ceiling on a single message. Default 8 MiB.
#
# Both at once, deliberately: the worst case either way is one message of this
# size in flight, so a second knob would only let the two disagree.
#
# It is what bounds peak memory. cf-imap materialises every attachment TWICE --
# decoded and base64 -- on top of the whole raw message held as a UTF-16 string,
# and offers no streaming API, so the resident cost of a fetch is several times
# what crossed the wire. A spike measured 2.54x for a SMALL text attachment, so
# treat that as a floor. 8 MiB against a Worker's ~128 MB leaves room for the
# multiple with the rest of the slice alongside it.
#
# A message above this is never body-fetched at all. Each range is preceded by
# one cheap header-only FETCH that answers RFC822.SIZE for every uid in it, and
# anything over the budget is recorded from those headers with `oversize` set:
# no body, no attachments, and no In-Reply-To or References either. It still
# gets a row, because gap detection counts rows -- skipping the message would
# leave its uid bucket permanently short and re-queue the range on every tick.
# SYNC_MAX_FETCH_BYTES=8388608

# Uids per enumeration SEARCH. Default 5000. Bounds the size of the response
# iCloud sends back and the wall clock of a single cron invocation.
SYNC_ENUMERATE_WINDOW=5000

# How many ranges one cron tick may queue, across all folders -- the throttle
# on a backfill. Default 50, so ~5000 messages an hour at the defaults above. A
# large folder therefore completes over several ticks. The ceiling is divided
# evenly between folders, so the first cannot starve the rest.
SYNC_MAX_CHUNKS_PER_RUN=50

# Optional ISO date. When set, only mail received on or after it is indexed --
# useful when a mailbox has a known floor. Dates are the only SEARCH criterion
# besides uid ranges that this worker will ever use: a spike found size and
# string criteria unusable against iCloud (LARGER matches everything, SMALLER
# nothing, and SUBJECT/TEXT/FROM return no hits at all).
# SYNC_SINCE=2022-03-01

# ---- Writes ----
#
# Both optional. The write tools cannot send and cannot delete, and they refuse
# Trash and Junk as move destinations. Every write, successful or not, lands in
# the write_log table. See "What the MCP server serves" in README.md.

# The address create_draft writes a draft From. Defaults to IMAP_USER when that
# is a full address -- on iCloud it usually is not, since LOGIN wants the local
# part only, and a draft with no From is legal. Apple Mail fills one in on send.
# DRAFT_FROM=you@example.com

# Where create_draft appends. Only needed when the mailbox advertises no
# \Drafts special-use AND has no folder called Drafts -- iCloud advertises no
# \Drafts, so the literal-name fallback is the path that normally runs.
# DRAFTS_FOLDER=Drafts

# ---- Secrets ----
#
# Never put these in the wrangler.jsonc `vars` block: that file is committed.
# Set them with `pnpm exec wrangler secret put <NAME>`, and locally in
# .dev.vars (gitignored -- copy .dev.vars.example).
#
# Both are declared required in wrangler.jsonc (`secrets.required`): a deploy
# fails, naming the missing ones, until they are set. On a FIRST deploy the
# Worker does not exist yet for `secret put` to target and the database does
# not exist to migrate, so the sequence is: pass the secrets alongside the
# deploy, then apply the schema —
#
#   cat > .secrets.env <<DONE        # gitignored
#   IMAP_PASSWORD=<app-specific password>
#   MCP_API_KEY=<output of: openssl rand -base64 32>
#   DONE
#   pnpm exec wrangler deploy --secrets-file .secrets.env
#   rm .secrets.env
#   pnpm run db:migrate:remote
#
# Every deploy after that is plain `pnpm run deploy`, which migrates and
# deploys in that order. scripts/setup-access.sh prints the same commands.
#
#   IMAP_PASSWORD   App-specific password. This grants FULL mailbox access
#                   including SMTP send, and must never reach a log line.
#   MCP_API_KEY     The bearer token MCP clients present in API-key mode.
#                   Generate one:  openssl rand -base64 32
#                   Rotation is replacing the secret:
#                   `pnpm exec wrangler secret put MCP_API_KEY`
